#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199039} :activity/id #uuid "f9f9eac5-9750-5e10-9a18-8a361f45282b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049170 :topic/id #uuid "e05008f8-83c0-57d4-bd73-7ea7692c3357" :topic/math-academy-id 4255} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199520 :tutorial/content "We can apply the logical operations of conjunction and disjunction to predicates.\n\n- The **conjunction** of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is a new predicate which we denote as follows: \n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\land Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)\n$$\n\nThis conjunction is true whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is true for a specific set of respective variable assignments. The conjunction is false if either $P$ or $Q$ is false.\n\n- The **disjunction** of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is written as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\lor Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)\n$$\n\nThis disjunction is true whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true, $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is true, or both are true, for a specific set of variable assignments. The disjunction is false if $P$ *and* $Q$ are false.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicates:\n\n- $P(n):$ $n$ is even\n\n- $Q(n):$ $n$ is positive\n\nThen, $P(n) \\land Q(n)$ is a new predicate that reads \"$n$ is even *and* $n$ is positive,\" which can be simplified to \"$n$ is even and positive.\"\n\nOn the other hand, $P(n) \\lor Q(n)$ is a new predicate that reads \"$n$ is even *or* $n$ is positive,\" which can be simplified to \"$n$ is even or positive.\"\n\nWhen it's clear that $P(n)$ is a predicate in the variable $n,$ we sometimes omit the variables when writing them. So, $P(n) \\land Q(n)$ and $P(n) \\lor Q(n)$ can be written as $P \\land Q$ and $P \\lor Q,$ respectively." :tutorial/id #uuid "c05b0e9a-b514-5c42-9d07-7ec2466b30be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7984 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b041ca76-9538-556f-ba42-756566d8edf6" :step/math-academy-id 120115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198252 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198099 :question/id #uuid "d40cb0df-fce7-5236-b0ef-62f5a343b3a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12724"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2993b4ab-e45b-579a-8a11-b5e9ee107d5b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048778}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575614}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"} :step/id #uuid "8701928d-1944-5363-b962-b4891f892daa" :step/math-academy-id 120116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198253 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198100 :question/id #uuid "3e2319b4-ab10-50ef-845b-9586c9761227" :question/math-academy-id "e-12725"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce659c55-ca26-52c6-b15e-ec317013d856" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575626}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Nested Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"} :step/id #uuid "77ba67c1-cc43-57be-ac98-62a769bb7e42" :step/math-academy-id 120119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199521 :tutorial/content "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nFrom the definition of the conjunction of predicates, we have that $P \\land Q$ is true if both $P$ and $Q$ are true. Therefore, the truth set of $P \\land Q$ is the *intersection* of the truth sets of $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{P \\land Q} = T_P \\cap T_Q\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicates defined over the set of positive integers:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $3$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $24$ is divisible by $x$\n\nWe start by finding the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x){:}$\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_P = \\big\\{ 3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27 \\ldots \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_Q = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P∧Q} & ={T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27…\\}\\cap \\{1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,12,24\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "74eb89b1-6654-56b5-9fef-718a3acfcb2f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7985 :tutorial/title "The Truth Set of a Conjunction"} :step/id #uuid "ea6a5438-89a7-508e-92fb-e311fd2c5c48" :step/math-academy-id 120122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199542 :tutorial/content "Once again, let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. \n\nFrom the definition of the disjunction of predicates, we have that $P \\lor Q$ is true if $P$ is true, or $Q$ is true, or both are true. Therefore, the truth set of $P \\lor Q$ must be the *union* of the truth sets of $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{P \\lor Q} = T_P \\cup T_Q\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the following shaded regions that represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y),$ defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ \n\n![](images/ce/ce71e62503acd1c82f6bba7e1d9c83c40f0a98f04c689c3ccd58e7a99a814856.png)\n\nTo get the truth set of $P(x,y) \\lor Q(x,y),$ we take all the points that belong to $T_P$ or $T_Q,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9c/9c528c5fe390bd1f22cb473c01b768e83f5b3a35d97eb7e670c6734b8cb2c2f8.png)\n\n**Note:** The points where the *dashed* line meets the *solid* curve belong to $T_P \\cup T_Q$ since these points lie in $T_P.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d1b797a-ff89-514d-a0df-87d6858b45e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8140 :tutorial/title "The Truth Set of a Disjunction"} :step/id #uuid "9905e635-bc99-5ab6-8557-95a009250e4e" :step/math-academy-id 120123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198254 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198101 :question/id #uuid "3a115dd5-3241-5627-bb40-121b75eeb7ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-12726"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6bc626a-9517-584c-bf55-8653227f830e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048592}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575613}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Sets of Conjunctions and Disjunctions"} :step/id #uuid "69f763bb-fe3d-5367-b28c-7de5d1163729" :step/math-academy-id 120124 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199523 :tutorial/content "Conjunction and disjunction of predicates are generalizations of logical operations on statements. Moreover, the properties of these operations on statements can be translated to corresponding operations on predicates. \n\nIn particular, the idempotent, absorption, associative, and commutative laws also apply to conjunction and disjunction of predicates. \n\nSo, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n),$ $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ and $R(z_1,z_2\\ldots,z_p),$ we have the following:\n\n- *The Idempotent Laws*: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧P≡P∨P≡P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Associative Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∧R) & ≡(P∧Q)∧R \\\\ P∨(Q∨R) & ≡(P∨Q)∨R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Commutative Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧Q & ≡Q∧P \\\\ P∨Q & ≡Q∨P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Distributive Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧R) \\\\ P∨(Q∧R) & ≡(P∨Q)∧(P∨R)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Absorption Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(P∨Q) & ≡P \\\\ P∨(P∧Q) & ≡P\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ad96982-4e36-52f9-816a-db64ba277433" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7989 :tutorial/title "Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction of Predicates"} :step/id #uuid "71a8a715-2397-5052-a148-b7cc79853a2b" :step/math-academy-id 120128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198255 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198104 :question/id #uuid "2ab89571-f4e2-515f-9d01-b1213e73ffc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f674fcf-7f3e-53c0-8759-6f83b0820160" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049824}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575606}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction"} :step/id #uuid "1887f6f2-227f-5082-89ee-2e84c56eb994" :step/math-academy-id 120129}] :activity/title "The \"And\" and \"Or\" Connectives With Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198514}]}
